<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Witbooi Nama</h1><p class="st-meta">1863 CE – 1905 CE (approx.) · capital Gibeon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Nama community of Gibeon led by Hendrik Witbooi, the most persistent opponent of German colonial rule in Namibia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Witboois, or Khowesin, were an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oorlam">Oorlam</a> group from the northern Cape. Around 1863 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kido-witbooi">Kido Witbooi</a> led them north from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pella">Pella</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibeon-namibia">Gibeon</a> in central southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, where a Rhenish mission was established and they became one of the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nama-people">Nama</a> communities.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-witbooi">Hendrik Witbooi</a>, grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kido-witbooi">Kido</a>, saw himself as a Christian leader with a divine mission and fought long campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herero-people">Herero</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and land. Literate and politically shrewd, he kept letters and a diary that are major sources. He refused German protection until a surprise German attack on his base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hornkranz">Hornkranz</a> in 1893 forced him to submit in 1894.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-witbooi">Witbooi</a> fought as a German ally for ten years, then rose against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a> in October 1904. He was killed in 1905, and the Witbooi were deported, many dying on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shark-island-concentration-camp">Shark Island</a>; his image appears on Namibian banknotes.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/witbooi-nama.jpg" alt="Witbooi Nama" width="320" height="450"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author; publisher Franz Spenker, Hamburg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hottentotten-H%C3%A4uptling_Witboy_mit_seiner_Familie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1863 CE – 1905 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gibeon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kido Witbooi, Moses Witbooi, Hendrik Witbooi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khoekhoe (Nama)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/namibia">Namibia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khowesin, Hendrik Witbooi's state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1884&amp;focus=witbooi-nama" aria-label="Witbooi Nama on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5151 3102 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Witbooi Nama lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5471 3358L5457 3267"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5457 3267h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5471 3358h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5471 3358h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gibeon. <a href="/atlas?year=1884&amp;focus=witbooi-nama">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

